  "body": "PR920 gives an eight-dimensional shared-frame bound for general norm-one\nlabels and a ten-dimensional positive example for triples. This contribution\nproves that the two-coordinate gap is necessary when both endpoint pairs\nare degenerate: signed scalar rank one plus disjoint binary spans forces\nthe source and target triples onto disjoint five-coordinate supports.\n\nThe all-dimension classification gives the exact count\n`225*C(h,5)*C(h-5,5)` with source/target sides distinguished, and a legal\ndimension-four frame for every rectangle in the class. The existing PR920\npositive fixtures and characteristic-vector argument are explicitly credited.\nThis is not an E8 puncture census, a new supplier, or a kappa improvement.\n\nValidation: canonical bit-mask census h5..12; independent complete labeled\nset/Fraction census h5..10 covering 6,930 source pairs and 615,060 eligible\nrectangles; all 15 sharp canonical h10 frames with 60 explicit orthonormal\nresidual bases; ten semantic checks; six package rejections and spaced-path\nrelocation. All three commands pass with isolated optimized Python.\n\nThe theorem is scoped to one scalar-shared node, triple labels, two\ndegenerate endpoint pairs, and the historical orthonormal-residual interface.\nIt does not supply a physical word, paid invoice, global DAG labeling or\nselected construction change. Fixed base ba82555c64e1879e4b7decce28186f208fcb8262.\nBy @woklerx with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance; Apache-2.0.\n",
